 </section><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="learn" id="dl-v3-cycle-1-learn"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Learn</h5><p>Uniform coverage of a wide numerical range wastes PN resolution on sparse tails. The encoding should follow sample density.</p>
 </section><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="redesign" id="dl-v3-cycle-1-redesign"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Improve</h5><p>We adopted quantile bins and exposed connection probability, APL inhibition, threshold adaptation, learning rate, weight cap and MBON inhibition as configurable settings. The reported SNN run peaks at 0.83, falls to 0.53 at cycle 400 and ends at 0.79; tuning did not remove training instability. See <a href="model.html#decoder-v3-uci-results">the full learning curve</a>.</p>
 </section></div><figure class="revision-visual" id="engineering-model-v3-figure-1"><img src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/model/a7671258c085-engineering-figure-1.webp" data-notion-source-block="40025656-1ff8-8104-a14b-d1085d78652b" alt="Equal-width and quantile PN encoding on UCI-270" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="2048" height="617"/><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 1. </strong>Equal-width and quantile PN encoding on UCI-270. The two projections show 11,128 training measurements; the readout bars use all 13,910 measurements in five-fold cross-validation. Bars are means and lines are the source-reported fold spread. These are linear PN-code readouts, not SNN scores.</figcaption></figure>
-</article><article class="eng-cycle eng-cycle--dry" data-cycle-panel="2" data-eng-cycle="" data-eng-cycle-num="2" data-eng-track="drylab" id="dl-v3-cycle-2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="dl-v3-cycle-2-tab"><header class="eng-cycle__head"><h4 class="eng-cycle__title reading-heading--detail"><span class="eng-cycle__num">Cycle 2</span> Locate the loss before redesigning the network</h4></header><div class="eng-cycle-context"><p class="eng-cycle-context__question">After parameter tuning, which representation limits the final decision?</p><p class="eng-cycle-context__status">Computational comparison</p></div><div class="eng-cycle__panels"><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="design" id="dl-v3-cycle-2-design"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Design</h5><p>We compared access to class information at every stage instead of diagnosing the model from its final score alone.</p>
+</article><article class="eng-cycle eng-cycle--dry" data-cycle-panel="2" data-eng-cycle="" data-eng-cycle-num="2" data-eng-track="drylab" id="dl-v3-cycle-2" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="dl-v3-cycle-2-tab" hidden=""><header class="eng-cycle__head"><h4 class="eng-cycle__title reading-heading--detail"><span class="eng-cycle__num">Cycle 2</span> Locate the loss before redesigning the network</h4></header><div class="eng-cycle-context"><p class="eng-cycle-context__question">After parameter tuning, which representation limits the final decision?</p><p class="eng-cycle-context__status">Computational comparison</p></div><div class="eng-cycle__panels"><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="design" id="dl-v3-cycle-2-design"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Design</h5><p>We compared access to class information at every stage instead of diagnosing the model from its final score alone.</p>
 </section><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="build" id="dl-v3-cycle-2-build"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Build</h5><p>For the 800-cycle UCI run, we reconstructed standardized features, PCA values and PN/KC/MBON spike counts for the recorded test presentations. Each representation received a linear-SVM probe. Repeated presentations of one measurement stayed in the same cross-validation fold.</p>
 </section><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="test" id="dl-v3-cycle-2-test"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Test</h5><p>The shared five-fold comparison contains 1,799 presentations from 1,322 measurements. Macro-F1 progresses from 0.97 (features) to 0.96 (PCA), 0.92 (PN), 0.89 (KC) and 0.79 (MBON). The network’s most-active-MBON answer also scores 0.79.</p>
 </section><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="learn" id="dl-v3-cycle-2-learn"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Learn</h5><p>The largest adjacent loss is at the KC→MBON readout. The KC representation supports a stronger linear classifier than the trained network uses.</p>
 </section><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="redesign" id="dl-v3-cycle-2-redesign"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Improve</h5><p>We made layerwise probes part of the comparison protocol and identified the learned readout as the next redesign target. A future readout change should be evaluated on fixed folds and independent runs; this cycle does not claim that such a redesign has already succeeded.</p>
 </section></div><figure class="revision-visual" id="engineering-model-v3-figure-2"><img src="https://static.igem.wiki/teams/6460/wiki/model/319fc5f3d9c2-model-figure-7.webp" data-notion-source-block="40025656-1ff8-8128-ad36-e7035823a4e2" alt="The same UCI layerwise comparison shown in Model Figure 7: five folds grouped by measurement over 1,799 presentations of 1,322 measurements, after 800 training cycles" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1925" height="661"/><figcaption><strong class="media-caption__label">Figure 2. </strong>UCI layerwise comparison, also shown in Model Figure 7. Five measurement-grouped folds cover 1,799 presentations from 1,322 measurements after 800 training cycles. Bars are means; lines show source-reported fold spread.</figcaption></figure>
-</article><article class="eng-cycle eng-cycle--dry" data-cycle-panel="3" data-eng-cycle="" data-eng-cycle-num="3" data-eng-track="drylab" id="dl-v3-cycle-3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="dl-v3-cycle-3-tab"><header class="eng-cycle__head"><h4 class="eng-cycle__title reading-heading--detail"><span class="eng-cycle__num">Cycle 3</span> Match coding resolution to receptor-data scale</h4></header><div class="eng-cycle-context"><p class="eng-cycle-context__question">Can the pipeline classify chemical families from the receptors used by AeroSense?</p><p class="eng-cycle-context__status">Computational comparison</p></div><div class="eng-cycle__panels"><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="design" id="dl-v3-cycle-3-design"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Design</h5><p>We moved from repeated metal-oxide gas measurements to published DoOR responses for the ten fly receptors in our cell-line panel. This changes the prediction task to chemical-family classification of distinct odorants.</p>
+</article><article class="eng-cycle eng-cycle--dry" data-cycle-panel="3" data-eng-cycle="" data-eng-cycle-num="3" data-eng-track="drylab" id="dl-v3-cycle-3" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="dl-v3-cycle-3-tab" hidden=""><header class="eng-cycle__head"><h4 class="eng-cycle__title reading-heading--detail"><span class="eng-cycle__num">Cycle 3</span> Match coding resolution to receptor-data scale</h4></header><div class="eng-cycle-context"><p class="eng-cycle-context__question">Can the pipeline classify chemical families from the receptors used by AeroSense?</p><p class="eng-cycle-context__status">Computational comparison</p></div><div class="eng-cycle__panels"><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="design" id="dl-v3-cycle-3-design"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Design</h5><p>We moved from repeated metal-oxide gas measurements to published DoOR responses for the ten fly receptors in our cell-line panel. This changes the prediction task to chemical-family classification of distinct odorants.</p>
 </section><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="build" id="dl-v3-cycle-3-build"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Build</h5><p>We retained 91 odorants measured on at least eight receptors, imputed missing values within training folds, and compared the SNN with logistic-regression readouts on the same five stratified odorant folds.</p>
 </section><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="test" id="dl-v3-cycle-3-test"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Test</h5><p>With twenty equal-width bins, the SNN scores 0.46 and a linear PN-code readout 0.52, compared with 0.69 from raw receptor values. Sixteen bins on the second component contain at most two odorants, while one contains 40. Five quantile bins increase the linear PN-code score to 0.66; the associated SNN configuration reaches 0.52.</p>
 </section><section class="eng-cycle__panel" data-eng-stage="learn" id="dl-v3-cycle-3-learn"><h5 class="eng-cycle__panel-title">Learn</h5><p>Fine bins fragment the small dataset. The SNN comparison also changes PN→KC probability from 0.05 to 0.2 and training duration from 200 to 100 cycles, so it does not isolate binning as the sole cause of improvement. The separate Test1 cell comparison does not yet establish agreement with DoOR.</p>
